# React 19 form event types

This project is on `react@19` with `@types/react@19.2+`, where
`React.FormEvent` and `React.FormEventHandler` are **deprecated**. The
documented replacements are `React.SubmitEvent` and `React.SubmitEventHandler`
(see `node_modules/@types/react/index.d.ts` — the deprecation JSDoc on
`FormEvent` literally points at `SubmitEvent`, and `HTMLAttributes.onSubmit` is
typed as `SubmitEventHandler<T>`).

Sources:
- DefinitelyTyped PR [#74383](https://github.com/DefinitelyTyped/DefinitelyTyped/pull/74383) ("deprecates `React.FormEvent` which has no counterpart in the DOM")
- typescript-cheatsheets/react [c299ceb](https://github.com/typescript-cheatsheets/react/commit/c299ceb2d0d26cdff06b2350b2ed6fd8d2449cc0) ("`FormEvent` and `FormEventHandler` are deprecated and should be replaced with `SubmitEvent` and `SubmitEventHandler`")

## Typing `onSubmit`

Prefer a named handler typed with `React.SubmitEvent<HTMLFormElement>`. Don't
wrap it in an anonymous `(event) => { event.preventDefault(); void fn(); }` just
to satisfy a lint rule — that splits `preventDefault` from the submit logic for
no reason.

```tsx
// BAD — deprecated type
const onSubmit: React.FormEventHandler<HTMLFormElement> = (e) => { /* ... */ };

// BAD — works, but loses the named/reusable handler and the accurate type
<form onSubmit={(event) => { event.preventDefault(); void handleSubmit(); }}>

// GOOD — matches how the onSubmit prop is typed in @types/react 19
async function onSubmit(event: React.SubmitEvent<HTMLFormElement>) {
  event.preventDefault();
  // ...
}

<form onSubmit={onSubmit}>
```

`React.SyntheticEvent<HTMLFormElement>` is accepted as a fallback by the
deprecation message, but `SubmitEvent` is more specific and is what the prop's
type actually is, so use `SubmitEvent`.

## When to use React 19 Actions instead

`useActionState` + `<form action={...}>` is the right pattern when the submit
does server work (Server Actions / server functions). For flows that call a
client SDK (e.g. `authClient.*` from `src/libs/auth-client.ts`) and then do
client-side routing or error mapping, a plain typed `onSubmit` handler is still
appropriate — don't migrate those to `useActionState` just because it's newer.
